🌍 Mars Living Systems Simulator (MLSS) — V4.1 Research-Backed Final Blueprint

Author & Principal Architect: Ouadi Maakoul

1. Executive Summary

The Mars Living Systems Simulator (MLSS) is a fully browser‑based, professional‑grade digital twin of a Martian colony, rigorously grounded in NASA‑STS‑7009B and related standards. It targets NASA engineering managers, systems engineers, physicists, and university students who need to prototype, test, and verify life‑support architectures under realistic constraints—without spreadsheets or custom scientific code.

MLSS integrates physics, human factors, biology, economics, and legal constraints into a single real‑time WebAssembly engine. Researchers can configure mission parameters, inject failures, compare human‑vs‑AI decision branches, and export SysML models for formal verification. The platform is designed to serve as a sandbox for conceptual mission design, human‑autonomy teaming research, and classroom education—all directly in the browser, with no physical testbed required.

1.1 Relationship to NASA-STD-7009B

NASA‑STD‑7009B establishes uniform practices for models and simulations developed and used in NASA programs: requirements for selection, application, design criteria, and acceptance standards. MLSS adopts these principles by:

· Documenting all abstractions, assumptions, and physical processes explicitly in a "Conceptual Model" manifest
· Maintaining a bijective mapping between simulated components and real aerospace hardware (digital thread)
· Enforcing deterministic replay and auditability through Merkle‑hashed event sourcing
· Requiring probabilistic closure gates (Monte Carlo runs) that serve as acceptance criteria
· Generating formal evidence artifacts (entropy audit logs, SysML exports) that could feed a Human‑Rating Certification Package

1.2 Reference Architecture

MLSS uses the NASA HOME project's digital twin framework as its architectural reference: a federated system of dissimilar ECLSS information models and simulation models that process semantic information about the habitat to inform, predict, and modify behavior.

2. Theoretical Foundation: The Multi‑Faceted Entropy Economy

2.1 Thermodynamic Entropy

All energy transformations are fully accounted:

\Delta S_{\text{generated}} = \frac{Q_{\text{waste}}}{T}

E_{\text{in}} = E_{\text{out}} + \Delta E_{\text{stored}} + T \cdot \Delta S_{\text{produced}}

The concept of atmospheric chemical entropy production derives from validated thermochemical models for the Martian atmosphere. Prior research has demonstrated that Mars possesses very little chemical disequilibrium compared to Earth, meaning the colony must actively inject free energy to maintain the ordered state required for life.

2.2 Mass Closure Score (Primary KPI)

\text{Mass Closure} = 1 - \frac{m_{\text{leaked}} + m_{\text{vented}}}{m_{\text{cycled}}}

MELiSSA stoichiometric modeling shows that full closure is achievable at steady state even for a crew of six: 12 out of 14 compounds exhibited zero loss in validated models. This is the gold standard for the simulation.

2.3 Cognitive Entropy

Poor decisions cost joules—tracked via the Regret Ledger. This operationalizes NASA's longstanding recognition that human trust in automation is a primary determinant of system performance, requiring explicit calibration.

2.4 Biological Entropy

Radiation dose causes double‑strand DNA breaks requiring ATP repair—modeled as a direct energy drain on crew metabolism. The Mars surface GCR environment is well‑characterized: MSL/RAD measured absorbed dose rates of ~0.21 mGy/day at solar max, increasing to ~0.31 mGy/day at solar minimum.

2.5 Economic Entropy

· 1 kg launched from Earth = 50,000 M$ (embodied rocket entropy)
· 1 kg from ISRU = 0.1 M$ + local energy cost
· This reflects real cost estimates for Martian ISRU, where producing 1 kg of propellant requires roughly 0.57 kg water and 8.62 kWh of energy, with overall system efficiencies around 50% when electrolysis losses are included

2.6 Mars Environmental Baseline (Research‑Derived Numbers)

Atmosphere:

· Mean surface pressure: 600 Pa (0.6 kPa)—less than 1% of Earth's
· Composition: 95.32% CO₂, 2.7% N₂, 1.6% Ar, 0.13% O₂
· CO₂ partial pressure at surface: ~570 Pa—available for ISRU capture

Temperature:

· Diurnal variation: 80–100 K swing between day and night
· MARSTHERM provides a validated seasonal/diurnal thermal model of the Martian near‑surface, available as a public web interface

Radiation:

· GCR dose rate: 0.21–0.31 mGy/day, varies by 80%+ with solar cycle
· Regolith shielding: first 15–20 cm provides the largest dose reduction; 1 m reduces primary particle dose by ~41% for a 30‑day stay

3. Core Mathematical Models (Research‑Calibrated)

3.1 Sabatier‑Electrolysis Loop

\text{CO}_2 + 4\text{H}_2 \rightarrow \text{CH}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \quad (\Delta H = -165\,\text{kJ/mol})

2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \quad (285.8\,\text{kJ/mol})

Operational parameters from ISRO experimental research: optimal Sabatier conditions at 1.5 bar, 550 K, using Ni‑catalyst packed‑bed reactor. The Sabatier‑electrolysis closed loop with recycle stream creates a complex nonlinear process dynamic (reactor‑separator with recycle). Per kilogram of O₂: ~20 MJ input with ~8 MJ low‑grade heat load.

3.2 CO₂ Scrubbing Capacity

NASA's Four‑Bed CO₂ Scrubber baseline: removes ~3.7 kg/day CO₂ at 2 mmHg partial pressure—one 4‑crew equivalent unit. On ISS, two CDRA units provide total capacity for up to 12 crew.

3.3 Water Recovery

ISS operations demonstrate recovery rates >90%, with the latest brine processor pushing overall rates to 98%. The urine processor achieves ~75% recovery, with the brine processor extracting the remaining water.

3.4 Human Metabolic Parameters

· O₂ consumption: ~0.84 kg/person/day at baseline
· Water requirement: ~2.2 L/person/day, increasing ~19% for larger body sizes
· Caloric intake: 2,000–2,500 kcal/day for women/men; ~10 MJ/person/day for men
· ISS astronauts typically consume only ~80% of daily requirements—a potential health risk for long‑duration missions

3.5 Crop Photosynthesis

Plants on Mars require 80–180 W/m² of photosynthetically active radiation (PAR), with optimal temperature 20–27 °C. Low‑pressure greenhouses enhance photosynthesis because gas diffusion rates increase, but introduce structural and leakage engineering challenges. Growth models use Monod kinetics as baseline.

3.6 Component Reliability (Weibull)

f(t; \lambda, k) = \frac{k}{\lambda} \left( \frac{t}{\lambda} \right)^{k-1} e^{-(t/\lambda)^k}

Parameters sourced from ISS ECLSS reliability data. Since the last comprehensive release in 2004, recent studies are integrating updated component replacement rates, shelf‑life, and throughput limits into new models. Weibull parameters extracted from published Mean Time Between Failure (MTBF) values for each component.

3.7 Radiation Dose Model

D = D_0 \cdot e^{-x/x_0} \cdot \text{SolarMod}

with D_0 = free‑space GCR dose rate (0.5–0.8 mGy/day at solar min), x_0 ≈ 20 g/cm² for regolith.

Empirical validation: RAD measurements show dose rate variation with atmospheric pressure and heliospheric conditions; simulation models agree to within ±20%. Full Monte Carlo codes (GEANT4, HZETRN) can be invoked optionally for higher‑fidelity analyses.

3.8 HCA Cognitive Dynamics

Stress Evolution:

\frac{dS}{dt} = a(1 - S)L - bS \cdot \text{Sleep} + c(1 - \text{Trust}) + \xi(t)

Parameters calibrated against HI‑SEAS biobehavioral data, which documented stress accumulation and contagion in 6‑person crews during 8–12 month Mars‑analog missions.

Social Contagion:

S_j \leftarrow S_j + \kappa_{ij}(S_i - S_j)

Contagion strength derived from observed team dynamic "third‑quarter" phenomenon—stress peaks and volatility typically occur in the middle third of isolation missions, consistent with depression and team friction patterns.

Override Probability:

P_{\text{override}} = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-(\beta_0 + \beta_1 S + \beta_2 (1-\text{Trust}) + \beta_3 \text{ThermalDiscomfort})}}

3.9 Skill Decay

s_k(t) = s_k(0) \cdot e^{-\lambda_{\text{decay}} \, t_{\text{unused}}}

λ_decay ≈ 0.0144/week (~1% per week) based on EVA training studies.

3.10 Environmental Stressors (Correlated)

Dust storm:

P_{\text{PV}} = P_0 (1 - 0.8\tau), \quad \eta_{\text{rad}} = \eta_0 (1 - 0.4\tau), \quad f_{\text{leak}} = 1 + 2\tau

Solar array degradation from dust is well‑documented: typical power loss of 0.2% per Sol, with observed rates spanning 0.05–2% per Sol depending on conditions. Opportunity rover data across 4.95 Martian years provides empirical soiling and degradation curves.

3.11 Communications (Delay‑Tolerant Resource)

Data staleness penalty:

\Delta S_{\text{cog}} \propto t_{\text{staleness}}^2

Policy upload cost: 2 MB. Below 10 kbps, full local autonomy is enforced—modeling the exact constraint that drives real Mars mission communication architecture design.

4. System Architecture

4.1 Architectural Overview

MLSS is structured as five synergistic engines—Life Support, MAAL, Environmental Stressor, Failure Cascade, and Radiation Biology—plus Counterfactual & Regret Infrastructure and a Multi‑Fidelity Visualization Layer. All engines run in a Web Worker (Rust→WASM), communicating asynchronously with the React/Three.js UI thread. This mirrors the federated digital twin framework developed by CMU for the NASA HOME project, where heterogeneous simulation and information models interoperate through shared semantic descriptions to inform, predict, and modify system behavior.

4.2 Entropy‑Aware Life Support Engine

Simulates O₂/CO₂ loops, water recovery, thermal management, and power grids. Every component is instantiated from a JSON Component Card (§6) with a digital thread to real hardware. This enabling approach is directly inspired by ESA's EcosimPro ECLSS library, which provides reusable components for multi‑disciplinary spacecraft life support modeling—covering fluid flow, chemical reactions, electrochemical processes, heat transfer, and biological systems.

4.3 Multi‑Agent Autonomous Layer (MAAL)

· Governor Agent: Long‑horizon resource optimizer; issues non‑binding recommendations and schedules training
· Specialist Agents: Manage O₂, water, power, thermal, and greenhouse subsystems; susceptible to SEU‑induced bit‑flips
· Worker Robots: Execute physical tasks (EVA repair, regolith moving) with battery/thermal constraints
· Human Crew Agents (HCAs): Virtual crew with full cognitive, social, biological, and skill models

HCAs respond to Earth‑side user overrides and may also act autonomously based on internal state. This directly addresses the challenge identified at NASA JSC—crew members are often the least modeled component despite being the primary source of variability.

4.4 Environmental Stressor Engine

Generates time‑varying dust storms (opacity, electrostatic), radiation (GCR + SPE), comm blackouts (bandwidth, BER), and supply‑chain shocks (missed launch windows, budget cuts). All stressors are correlated; a single dust event impacts multiple subsystems simultaneously.

4.5 Failure Cascade & Cyber‑Physical Risk Graph

A typed dependency graph (Physical, Information, Software, Social, Economic, Legal) enables real‑time cascade visualization. Information edges model SEU‑induced sensor corruption; Social edges model panic contagion; Legal edges flag COSPAR violations.

4.6 Radiation Biology Engine

Tracks dose to each HCA based on shielding geometry, solar activity, and EVA exposure. MSL/RAD empirical data is used for baseline dose rates, with validated models (agreement within ±20%) for atmospheric and solar modulation—providing scientific confidence in the radiation environment simulation.

4.7 Counterfactual & Regret Infrastructure

Every tick is Merkle‑hashed into an append‑only event store (IndexedDB). Every manual override auto‑branches into an AI‑recommended path. The Regret Ledger displays ΔMassClosure, ΔEntropy, ΔM$. This infrastructure is designed to produce evidence artifacts compliant with NASA‑STD‑7009B acceptance criteria, enabling the simulation to contribute to formal verification processes.

